[Music]  so today I'd like to talk to you about  what I call public risk management I  came up with using that term to avoid  the much more scary term regulation so  today's topic is gonna be talking about  public risk management for AI the path  forward and I'm actually going to  discuss quite a bit why I don't think  traditional regulation is going to work  and why I'm optimistic that that's okay  and that we're going to be able to  manage the risks associated with AI  without the need for direct government  intervention so let's jump right into it  what is a public risk well public risk  is a term coined by Peter Huber in the  1980s that I think in an article that I  think I'm the only person who read and  but it really struck a chord with me it  refers to potential sources of harm that  are centrally or mass-produced  or widely distributed and that are  largely outside the control of the  individual risk bearer so what does that  mean in plain English a public risk is  something that could potentially harm a  lot of people but the individual  potential victims have no way of  stopping the harm from happening so  examples of public risks that you all  would be familiar with include nuclear  technology mass-produced consumer goods  and environmental threats and mechanized  transportation these are all things that  meet those criteria above and I think  that many autonomous systems are going  to share those same features their  autonomous systems are becoming  increasingly ubiquitous in our lives but  there are technologies that most people  who use them are not going to have a  hand in their creation and are not going  to really be able to control the safety  aspects of them unless they are well  designed so that they can do so so as  you might have noticed from that list of  different forms of public risks public  risks didn't really exist prior to  industrialization at least of the human  generated variety so this is a  relatively recent phenomenon the concept  of public risk so the modern forms of  public risk management what we think of  as regulation were developed in response  to and because the fact that  pre-industrial institutions proved to be  fundamentally incapable of may  the risks and harms generated by  industrialization and there's a number  of social and economic reasons for that  but I think that everybody can agree  that by and large in the industrial era  there was a great centralization of  production of different of different  goods and services that people use and  that centralization meant that unlike  back in the day when your shoes were  made by the local cobbler and your toys  were made in your house they were made  by people thousands or hundreds of miles  away and you had no way of making sure  that what they were doing when they were  making that shoe and when they were  making that toy was safe so what we  ended up developing are a number of  different forms of risk management and  I've kind of created two axes here to  categorize them there's formal and  informal methods of risk management and  you could also call those governmental  and non-governmental and then  pre-emptive and reactive and you could  also think of those as ex ante and ex  post or immediate things that take  immediate effect and those things that  take time to kind of develop and usually  when people think of regulation they  think of the top two things in the top  left cornor legislation and agency  regulation I'm gonna spend today talking  about the different things on this chart  and why it's generally I think that it's  going to be challenging for these forms  of risk management to manage the risk  associated with AI the one thing we're  not going to talk about further that's  on this list is subsidies and that's  both because that is a not very coercive  and not very direct method of managing  the risk associated with technology and  also because I don't get the impression  that in the world of artificial  intelligence 