gy research and it is hard to  mass-produce consumer goods without  leaving a large physical footprint of  some kind there other traces and  footprints in the sand that regulators  can use to figure out where the harm is  coming from and who is responsible for  generating it that's not necessarily  going to be the case with artificial  intelligence  as with other Information Age  technologies you can do work on an  artificial intelligence system without  leaving a large physical footprint  without large  visible infrastructure in place there's  also a discreteness with an ete problem  and this form of discreteness refers to  the fact that the risk might stem from  the interaction of components that are  created at different times in places  without conscious coordination think of  the machine learning libraries on github  if those get incorporated into a system  it's kind of hard to say that there was  one person who is responsible for  designing that machine learning library  and it's even more difficult to say  excuse me you can't even necessarily  hold them collectively responsible  because they may not have coordinated  with one another in any sort of  conscious way commits to github can  obviously be made without the person who  makes one commit having checked with the  person who made the previous commit to  see if they're okay with it so the next  issue is diffuseness this is closely  related to that last issue of  discreteness and that refers to the fact  that designers and manufacturers of  components and the people who have input  into coding may be in different  jurisdictions and the operators of the  technologies might end up being in yet  other jurisdictions the last issue is  opacity and that's exactly what it  sounds like and that simply means that  regulators may not be able to discover  or understand the underlying mechanisms  that create the risk so these problems  are what led me to question the ability  of traditional formal regulation to  manage the risks associated with AI well  why can't we just leave it to the free  market why not just let consumers go and  buy AI products and then those that end  up killing members of their family don't  get purchased again in the future well  there's a few problems with that and as  you'll see these are issues that are not  at all a new phenomenon as far as  challenges for free market risk  management one is information asymmetry  and I place a lot of weight on this one  personally producers have more  information about the potential sources  of risk in their products than consumers  do I have a feeling that this is going  to be particularly cute with it acute  with AI just as it is for other emerging  technologies when the technology is new  usually the people who are creating that  tech  the innovators are the only people who  are going to have a full proper  understanding of how it works so and  certainly consumers who have not  interacted with that technology before  are not going to have an proper  understanding of the underlying  mechanisms by which it works that allows  them to assess the risk associated with  it  so that information asymmetry that's a  failure in the free market that results  from inter excuse me back up failures in  the free market that resulted from  information asymmetry are a large part  of why the regulatory state was  developed it was meant to be some great  equalizer that allowed consumers to be  protected because there are particular  government agencies and institutions  that did have the knowledge about the  public risks and that we're able to  develop rules that were able to manage  those risks even if the individual  potential victims didn't have a full  understanding so what about insurance  this is something that I've struggled  with a little bit I think that certainly  insurance offers some promise as a  method of risk management for AI but the  problem is that it's difficult to  estimate risks associated with new  technologies 