t waiting for the harm to  occur before making the move in  assessing the risks associated with  autonomous systems that's pretty unusual  for new technologies usually producers  don't want to go out ahead and create  the perception that there might be risks  associated with their technologies by  preemptively discussing the safety  aspects and the safety challenges that  it presents we're seeing a level of  awareness of that that with AI that you  haven't seen with earlier generations of  public risks and this is reflected in  private sector initiatives encouraging  transparency and beneficial AI such as  the partnership on AI the open AI  project and we also have broad  stakeholder participation in these  initiatives and the AI community has so  far been very welcoming of input from  policy ethics and legal experts and  those stakeholders are often having a  direct role in those current initiatives  that are underway that means that the  industry standards  it could be developed might align better  with those of society at large than  traditionally is the case with industry  standards and self-regulation and also  there's an emerging consensus around the  goals of what we want to see as far as  beneficial artificial intelligence and  that was beautifully reflected with the  survey results yesterday so there's an  additional reason that I'm optimistic  though those things by themselves would  be good but when industry standards have  a strong degree of consensus they can be  amplified by the common law and I'm  going to try to summarize what the  common law is in about 15 seconds and  that's not easy but the common law is a  system where legal rules don't come from  fixed legislation but instead develop  organically from precedents derived from  the resolution of individual disputes so  if in one dispute they say you have to  clean up a spill on aisle four within 10  minutes and if you don't you're  negligent then the next court that comes  along is going to look at that prior  case and say well you didn't clean up  that spill on aisle four within 10  minutes so you were negligent and what  this allows is kind of a slow  development of legal rules about what  behavior is acceptable and what behavior  is not this is the dominant legal system  in the english-speaking world and  elements of the common law are becoming  increasingly prevalent in a number of  other countries and the key mechanism  for the purposes of risk management with  emerging technologies is tort law tort  law is a broad area of the law and one  feature of it is that all people have a  duty of care to avoid causing harm when  they're engaging in activities that  could potentially lead to harm when true  industry consensus emerges and those  that consensus is reflected in written  standards those standards can  effectively become the law through the  adoption by common law courts as part of  that duty of care to avoid causing harm  that hasn't happened as often as I think  should have over the course of the  industrial era in part because that  industry consensus is very hard to  achieve and it rarely emerges early  enough for it to be incorporated before  legislation and age  see regulation preempts the field I want  to talk a little bit about some current  developments that are going on that are  also specifically giving me hope that  there's actually movement happening with  industry standards that could lead to  effective risk management for AI a lot  of them are happening at the I Triple E  the I Triple E has a global initiative  for ethical considerations in AI and  autonomous systems it's an initiative  that is divided and committees focusing  on different aspects of ensuring  ethically aligned design they produced a  draft version of a draft version of  basically the goals and potential  standards that the initiative could lead  to last month and that is currently in  the public comment period and this is  the time to engage and provide input on  what 