olders worldwide we're also seeing  a number of sector specific initiatives  in areas such as autonomous weapons and  autonomous vehicles  so in closing the momentum that is being  generated by this conference and others  like it must not only be sustained but  intensified we can't stop with general  statements of principles we have to be  concrete about the goals that we want to  achieve and we have to be concrete about  what it means to have safe and  beneficial AI this is a rare opportunity  here to shape policy without going to a  government for approval so the bottom  line is this is I I think it's easy to  lose sight at these conferences of what  a rare opportunity there is here this is  the most important invention arguably in  the history of mankind and the people in  this room have the ability to shape the  way that policy develops for that  technology that's in once in in more  than a generation that's once in  multiple lifetimes opportunity so don't  fear law policy and ethics people engage  them and engage with each other that's a  great overview Matt thanks very much and  one thing you didn't touch on was that  dynamic with society in general and you  know we've learnt over the last 15 20  years that the social dynamic social  trust social accountability are critical  in terms of creating barriers for  technology innovation or enabling it you  just say a little bit about  how you see that aspect of things around  engagement trust and accountability fit  again with your governance ideas well  one thing that we do have to bear in  mind that these conferences is that we  are to a certain degree breathing  rarefied air you know the broader the  the man on the street as it were the  people who say elected Trump is  president or not in this room by and  large and we need to bear that in mind  when we're thinking about how society is  going to react to the standards that we  have and so I guess I should have added  to my talk and say we shouldn't just  engage 